000002,-16.933637758660268 228,-17.751788020873136 228.4,-18.57193828308607 228.79999999999998,-19.394088545298928 229.20000000000002,-20.21823880751191 229.60000000000002,-21.044389069724843 230,-21.87253933193773 230.39999999999998,-22.702689594150627 230.79999999999998,-23.534839856363533 231.20000000000002,-24.368990118576505 231.6,-25.205140380789402 232,-26.043290643002308 232.39999999999998,-26.883440905215224 232.8,-27.725591167428206 233.2,-28.569741429641084 233.6,-29.41589169185397 234,-30.26404195406687 234.4,-31.11419221627986 234.8,-31.96634247849275 235.2,-32.820492740705674 235.6,-33.67664300291858 236,-34.534793265131555 236.4,-35.394943527344424 236.8,-36.25709378955733 237.2,-37.12124405177022 237.60000000000002,-37.9873943139832 238,-38.85554457619611 238.4,-39.725694838409 238.79999999999998,-40.597845100621896 239.20000000000002,-41.47199536283489 239.60000000000002,-42.34814562504778 240,-43.226295887260704 240.39999999999998,-44.106446149473584 240.79999999999998,-44.98859641168647 241.20000000000002,-45.87274667389946 241.6,-46.758896936112365 242,-47.647047198325254 242.39999999999998,-48.53719746053815 242.8,-49.42934772275112 243.2,-50.323497984964035 243.6,-51.219648247176934 244,-52.117798509389814 244.4,-53.01794877160279 244.8,-53.92009903381569 245.2,-54.8242492960286 245.6,-55.730399558241515 246,-56.6385498204545 246.4,-57.54870008266738 246.8,-58.46085034488027 247.2,-59.37500060709317 247.60000000000002,-60.291150869306165 248,-61.209301131519055 248.4,-62.12945139373198 248.79999999999998,-63.051601655944864 249.20000000000002,-63.97575191815781 249.60000000000002,-64.90190218037074 250,-65.83005244258362\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></polyline><circle cx=\"170\" cy=\"80\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></circle><rect x=\"146\" y=\"0\" width=\"48\" height=\"160\" fill=\"hsla(189, 100%, 50%, 10%)\"></rect><rect x=\"50\" y=\"40\" width=\"160\" height=\"80\" fill=\"hsla(180, 100%, 25%, 10%)\"></rect><foreignObject x=\"112\" y=\"16\" width=\"320\" height=\"80\" fill=\"#ff0000\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math></div></foreignObject></svg><figcaption>If <math><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> and <math><mrow><mi>|</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>|</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math>, then <math><mrow><mi>|</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>|</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></math>.</figcaption></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"If ``\\varepsilon`` is any strict positive number, *no matter how small*, we must be able to ensure that ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon`` by restricting ``x`` to be *close enough* to (but not equal to) ``a``. How close is close enough? It is sufficient that the distance ``\\left|x-a\\right|`` from ``x`` to ``a`` be less than a positive number ``\\delta`` that depends on ``\\varepsilon``.\n\n!!! definition \"formal\"\n    We say that ``f:X\\mapsto Y`` approaches the limit ``L`` as ``x`` tends to ``a``, and we write\n    \n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L\n    ```\n\n    if the following condition is satisfied:\n    \n    ```math\n    \\forall\\varepsilon>0,\\exists\\delta\\left(\\varepsilon\\right)>0:0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta\\implies x\\in X \\wedge \\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n    ```\n\nNote the possible dependency of ``\\delta`` on ``\\varepsilon`` and the fact that ``x`` belongs to the domain of ``f``.\n\nThe formal definition of limit does not tell you how to find the limit of a function, but it does enable you to verify that a suspected limit is correct.\n\n!!! example\n    Verify that:\n\n    1. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}x=a``.\n\n       Let ``\\varepsilon>0`` be given. We must find ``\\delta>0`` so that\n\n       ```math\n       0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta\\implies\\left|x-a\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n       ```\n\n       Clearly, we can take ``\\delta=\\varepsilon`` and the implication above will be true. This proves that ``\\lim_{x\\to a}x=a``\n\n    2. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}c=c`` (``c`` is a constant).\n\n       Let ``\\varepsilon>0`` be given. We must find ``\\delta>0`` so that\n\n       ```math\n       0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta\\implies\\left|c-c\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n       ```\n\n       Since ``c-c=0``, we can use any positive number for ``\\delta`` and the implication above will be true. This proves that ``\\lim_{x\\to a}c=c``.\n\n    3. ``\\lim_{x\\to 2}x^2=4``.\n\n       Here ``a=2`` and ``L=4``. Let ``\\varepsilon>0`` be given. We must find ``\\delta>0`` so that\n\n       ```math\n       0<\\left|x-2\\right|<\\delta\\implies\\left|x^2-4\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n       ```\n\n       Now,\n\n       ```math\n       \\left|x^2-4\\right|=\\left|\\left(x+2\\right)\\left(x-2\\right)\\right|=\\left|x+2\\right|\\left|x-2\\right|\\,.\n       ```\n\n       We want the expression above to be less than ``\\varepsilon``. We can make the factor ``\\left|x-2\\right|`` as small as we wish by choosing ``\\delta`` properly, but we need to control the factor ``\\left|x+2\\right|`` so that it does not become too large.\n\n       If we first assume ``\\delta\\le1`` and require that ``\\left|x-2\\right|<\\delta``, then we have\n\n       ```math\n       \\left|x-2\\right|<1 \\implies 1<x<3 \\implies 3<x+2<5 \\implies \\left|x+2\\right|<5\n       ```\n\n       Hence,\n\n       ```math\n       \\left|f\\left(x\\right)-4\\right|<5\\left|x-2\\right|\\quad\\textrm{if}\\quad\\left|x-2\\right|<\\delta\\le1\\,.\n       ```\n\n       But ``5\\left|x-2\\right|<\\varepsilon`` if ``\\left|x-2\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{5}``. Therefore, if we take ``\\delta=\\min\\left\\lbrace1,\\frac{\\varepsilon}{5}\\right\\rbrace``, the *minimum* of the two numbers ``1`` and ``\\frac{\\varepsilon}{5}``, then\n\n       ```math\n       \\left|f\\left(x-4\\right)\\right|<5\\left|x-2\\right|<5\\times\\frac{\\varepsilon}{5}=\\varepsilon\\quad\\textrm{if}\\quad\\left|x-2\\right|<\\delta\\,.\n       ```\n\n       This proves that ``\\lim_{x\\to 2}x^2=4``.\n\nWe do not usually rely on the formal definition of limit to verify specific limits such as those in the last example. Rather, we appeal to general theorems about limits in particular the theorems of the next section.\n\nIf a function has a limit at a point, this limit is *unique*.\n\n!!! theorem\n    If ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L`` and ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=M``, then ``L=M``.\n\n!!! proof \"by contradiction\"\n    Suppose ``L\\ne M``.\n\n    Let ``\\varepsilon = \\frac{\\left|L-M\\right|}{2}>0``. \n    \n    Since ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L``, there exists ``\\delta_1 > 0`` such that\n\n    ```math\n    0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_1\\implies\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon\n    ```\n\n    and since ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=M``, there exists ``\\delta_2>0`` such that\n\n    ```math\n    0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_2\\implies\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n    ```\n\n    Let ``\\delta=\\min\\left\\lbrace\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\right\\rbrace``. If ``0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta``, then ``\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_1``, so ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon``, and ``\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_2``, so ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|<\\varepsilon``. Therefore,\n\n    ```math\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\left|L-M\\right|=&\\left|L-f\\left(x\\right)+f\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|\\\\\\le&\\left|L-f\\left(x\\right)\\right|+\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|\\\\<&2\\varepsilon=2\\frac{\\left|L-M\\right|}{2}=\\left|L-M\\right|\n    \\end{aligned}\n    ```\n\n    by the triangle inequality.\n\n    This is a contradiction, so ``L=M``.\n\nAlthough a function ``f`` can only have one limit at any particular point, it is, nevertheless, useful to be able to describe the behaviour of functions that approach different numbers a ``x`` tends to ``a`` from one side or the other.","output":"","result":"If ``\\varepsilon`` is any strict positive number, *no matter how small*, we must be able to ensure that ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon`` by restricting ``x`` to be *close enough* to (but not equal to) ``a``. How close is close enough? It is sufficient that the distance ``\\left|x-a\\right|`` from ``x`` to ``a`` be less than a positive number ``\\delta`` that depends on ``\\varepsilon``.\n\n!!! definition \"formal\"\n    We say that ``f:X\\mapsto Y`` approaches the limit ``L`` as ``x`` tends to ``a``, and we write\n    \n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L\n    ```\n\n    if the following condition is satisfied:\n    \n    ```math\n    \\forall\\varepsilon>0,\\exists\\delta\\left(\\varepsilon\\right)>0:0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta\\implies x\\in X \\wedge \\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n    ```\n\nNote the possible dependency of ``\\delta`` on ``\\varepsilon`` and the fact that ``x`` belongs to the domain of ``f``.\n\nThe formal definition of limit does not tell you how to find the limit of a function, but it does enable you to verify that a suspected limit is correct.\n\n!!! example\n    Verify that:\n\n    1. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}x=a``.\n\n       Let ``\\varepsilon>0`` be given. We must find ``\\delta>0`` so that\n\n       ```math\n       0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta\\implies\\left|x-a\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n       ```\n\n       Clearly, we can take ``\\delta=\\varepsilon`` and the implication above will be true. This proves that ``\\lim_{x\\to a}x=a``\n\n    2. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}c=c`` (``c`` is a constant).\n\n       Let ``\\varepsilon>0`` be given. We must find ``\\delta>0`` so that\n\n       ```math\n       0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta\\implies\\left|c-c\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n       ```\n\n       Since ``c-c=0``, we can use any positive number for ``\\delta`` and the implication above will be true. This proves that ``\\lim_{x\\to a}c=c``.\n\n    3. ``\\lim_{x\\to 2}x^2=4``.\n\n       Here ``a=2`` and ``L=4``. Let ``\\varepsilon>0`` be given. We must find ``\\delta>0`` so that\n\n       ```math\n       0<\\left|x-2\\right|<\\delta\\implies\\left|x^2-4\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n       ```\n\n       Now,\n\n       ```math\n       \\left|x^2-4\\right|=\\left|\\left(x+2\\right)\\left(x-2\\right)\\right|=\\left|x+2\\right|\\left|x-2\\right|\\,.\n       ```\n\n       We want the expression above to be less than ``\\varepsilon``. We can make the factor ``\\left|x-2\\right|`` as small as we wish by choosing ``\\delta`` properly, but we need to control the factor ``\\left|x+2\\right|`` so that it does not become too large.\n\n       If we first assume ``\\delta\\le1`` and require that ``\\left|x-2\\right|<\\delta``, then we have\n\n       ```math\n       \\left|x-2\\right|<1 \\implies 1<x<3 \\implies 3<x+2<5 \\implies \\left|x+2\\right|<5\n       ```\n\n       Hence,\n\n       ```math\n       \\left|f\\left(x\\right)-4\\right|<5\\left|x-2\\right|\\quad\\textrm{if}\\quad\\left|x-2\\right|<\\delta\\le1\\,.\n       ```\n\n       But ``5\\left|x-2\\right|<\\varepsilon`` if ``\\left|x-2\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{5}``. Therefore, if we take ``\\delta=\\min\\left\\lbrace1,\\frac{\\varepsilon}{5}\\right\\rbrace``, the *minimum* of the two numbers ``1`` and ``\\frac{\\varepsilon}{5}``, then\n\n       ```math\n       \\left|f\\left(x-4\\right)\\right|<5\\left|x-2\\right|<5\\times\\frac{\\varepsilon}{5}=\\varepsilon\\quad\\textrm{if}\\quad\\left|x-2\\right|<\\delta\\,.\n       ```\n\n       This proves that ``\\lim_{x\\to 2}x^2=4``.\n\nWe do not usually rely on the formal definition of limit to verify specific limits such as those in the last example. Rather, we appeal to general theorems about limits in particular the theorems of the next section.\n\nIf a function has a limit at a point, this limit is *unique*.\n\n!!! theorem\n    If ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L`` and ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=M``, then ``L=M``.\n\n!!! proof \"by contradiction\"\n    Suppose ``L\\ne M``.\n\n    Let ``\\varepsilon = \\frac{\\left|L-M\\right|}{2}>0``. \n    \n    Since ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L``, there exists ``\\delta_1 > 0`` such that\n\n    ```math\n    0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_1\\implies\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon\n    ```\n\n    and since ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=M``, there exists ``\\delta_2>0`` such that\n\n    ```math\n    0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_2\\implies\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n    ```\n\n    Let ``\\delta=\\min\\left\\lbrace\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\right\\rbrace``. If ``0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta``, then ``\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_1``, so ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon``, and ``\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_2``, so ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|<\\varepsilon``. Therefore,\n\n    ```math\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\left|L-M\\right|=&\\left|L-f\\left(x\\right)+f\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|\\\\\\le&\\left|L-f\\left(x\\right)\\right|+\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|\\\\<&2\\varepsilon=2\\frac{\\left|L-M\\right|}{2}=\\left|L-M\\right|\n    \\end{aligned}\n    ```\n\n    by the triangle inequality.\n\n    This is a contradiction, so ``L=M``.\n\nAlthough a function ``f`` can only have one limit at any particular point, it is, nevertheless, useful to be able to describe the behaviour of functions that approach different numbers a ``x`` tends to ``a`` from one side or the other.","resultHTML":"<p>If <math><mi>ε</mi></math> is any strict positive number, <em>no matter how small</em>, we must be able to ensure that <math><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></math> by restricting <math><mi>x</mi></math> to be <em>close enough</em> to (but not equal to) <math><mi>a</mi></math>. How close is close enough? It is sufficient that the distance <math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow></math> from <math><mi>x</mi></math> to <math><mi>a</mi></math> be less than a positive number <math><mi>δ</mi></math> that depends on <math><mi>ε</mi></math>.</p>\n<aside class=\"markdown-admonition markdown-admonition-definition\"><div class=\"markdown-admonition-title\">Definition: formal</div><div class=\"markdown-admonition-body\"><p>We say that <math><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace=\"0.2222em\" rspace=\"0.2222em\">:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>↦</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></math> approaches the limit <math><mi>L</mi></math> as <math><mi>x</mi></math> tends to <math><mi>a</mi></math>, and we write</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>if the following condition is satisfied:</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mi>∀</mi><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>∃</mi><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace=\"0.2222em\" rspace=\"0.2222em\">:</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math></div></aside>\n<p>Note the possible dependency of <math><mi>δ</mi></math> on <math><mi>ε</mi></math> and the fact that <math><mi>x</mi></math> belongs to the domain of <math><mi>f</mi></math>.</p>\n<p>The formal definition of limit does not tell you how to find the limit of a function, but it does enable you to verify that a suspected limit is correct.</p>\n<aside class=\"markdown-admonition markdown-admonition-example\"><div class=\"markdown-admonition-title\">Example</div><div class=\"markdown-admonition-body\"><p>Verify that:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>.\n<p>Let <math><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> be given. We must find <math><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> so that</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>Clearly, we can take <math><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></math> and the implication above will be true. This proves that <math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math></p>\n</li>\n<li><math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> (<math><mi>c</mi></math> is a constant).\n<p>Let <math><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> be given. We must find <math><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> so that</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>Since <math><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, we can use any positive number for <math><mi>δ</mi></math> and the implication above will be true. This proves that <math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n</li>\n<li><math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>.\n<p>Here <math><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and <math><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>. Let <math><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> be given. We must find <math><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> so that</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>Now,</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>We want the expression above to be less than <math><mi>ε</mi></math>. We can make the factor <math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow></math> as small as we wish by choosing <math><mi>δ</mi></math> properly, but we need to control the factor <math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow></math> so that it does not become too large.\n\n   If we first assume <math><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and require that <math><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math>, then we have</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mn>1</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mn>3</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>5</mn><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>\n<p>Hence,</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>5</mn><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mtext>if</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>But <math><mrow><mn>5</mn><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></math> if <math><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mi>ε</mi><mn>5</mn></mfrac></mrow></math>. Therefore, if we take <math><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mfrac><mi>ε</mi><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">}</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, the <em>minimum</em> of the two numbers <math><mn>1</mn></math> and <math><mfrac><mi>ε</mi><mn>5</mn></mfrac></math>, then</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>5</mn><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>×</mo><mfrac><mi>ε</mi><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>ε</mi><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mtext>if</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>This proves that <math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n</li>\n</ol></div></aside>\n<p>We do not usually rely on the formal definition of limit to verify specific limits such as those in the last example. Rather, we appeal to general theorems about limits in particular the theorems of the next section.</p>\n<p>If a function has a limit at a point, this limit is <em>unique</em>.</p>\n<aside class=\"markdown-admonition markdown-admonition-theorem\"><div class=\"markdown-admonition-title\">Theorem</div><div class=\"markdown-admonition-body\"><p>If <math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and <math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>, then <math><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"markdown-admonition markdown-admonition-proof\"><div class=\"markdown-admonition-title\">Proof: by contradiction</div><div class=\"markdown-admonition-body\"><p>Suppose <math><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Let <math><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. </p>\n<p>Since <math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, there exists <math><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> such that</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>and since <math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>, there exists <math><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> such that</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>Let <math><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">{</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">}</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. If <math><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math>, then <math><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>, so <math><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></math>, and <math><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>, so <math><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></math>. Therefore,</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mtable displaystyle=\"true\" class=\"tml-jot\"><mtr><mtd class=\"tml-right\" style=\"padding-left:0em;padding-right:0em;\"><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class=\"tml-left\" style=\"padding-left:0em;padding-right:0em;\"><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class=\"tml-right\" style=\"padding-left:0em;padding-right:0em;\"><mo>≤</mo></mtd><mtd class=\"tml-left\" style=\"padding-left:0em;padding-right:0em;\"><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class=\"tml-right\" style=\"padding-left:0em;padding-right:0em;\"><mo>&lt;</mo></mtd><mtd class=\"tml-left\" style=\"padding-left:0em;padding-right:0em;\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>ε</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></math>\n<p>by the triangle inequality.</p>\n<p>This is a contradiction, so <math><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>.</p></div></aside>\n<p>Although a function <math><mi>f</mi></math> can only have one limit at any particular point, it is, nevertheless, useful to be able to describe the behaviour of functions that approach different numbers a <math><mi>x</mi></math> tends to <math><mi>a</mi></math> from one side or the other.</p>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const figure = JSCells.figure({\n    width: 255,\n    height: 155,\n    caption: \"\\\\(\\\\lim_{x\\\\to0^-}\\\\operatorname{sgn} x=-1\\\\) and \\\\(\\\\lim_{x\\\\to0^+}\\\\operatorname{sgn} x=1\\\\).\",\n});\n\nfigure.axes({\n    origin: [125, 80],\n    scale: 50,\n    x: { ticks: [-2, -1, 1, 2] },\n    y: { ticks: [-1, 1] },\n});\n\nfigure.plot(() => -1, {\n    domain: [-3, 0],\n    samples: 2,\n    stroke: \"#ff0000\",\n});\nfigure.plot(() => 1, {\n    domain: [0, 3],\n    samples: 2,\n    stroke: \"#ff0000\",\n});\n\nfigure.point(0, -1, {\n    radius: 3,\n    fill: \"#ffffff\",\n    stroke: \"#ff0000\",\n});\nfigure.point(0, 1, {\n    radius: 3,\n    fill: \"#ffffff\",\n    stroke: \"#ff0000\",\n});\n\nfigure.label(\"O\", { x: 109, y: 80, latex: true, width: 14, height: 18 });\n\nfigure;","output":"","result":"xy−2−112−11Olimx→0−⁡sgn⁡x=−1 and limx→0+⁡sgn⁡x=1.","resultHTML":"<figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"255\" height=\"155\" viewBox=\"0 0 255 155\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"255\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><line x1=\"125\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"125\" y2=\"155\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"255,80 248,76 248,84\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><polygon points=\"125,0 121,7 129,7\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><foreignObject x=\"241\" y=\"50\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"133\" y=\"-2\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>y</mi></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"25\" y1=\"76\" x2=\"25\" y2=\"84\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"21\" y=\"88\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"75\" y1=\"76\" x2=\"75\" y2=\"84\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"71\" y=\"88\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"175\" y1=\"76\" x2=\"175\" y2=\"84\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"171\" y=\"88\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"225\" y1=\"76\" x2=\"225\" y2=\"84\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"221\" y=\"88\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mn>2</mn></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"121\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"129\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"101\" y=\"118\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"121\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"129\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"101\" y=\"18\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></div></foreignObject><polyline points=\"-25,130 125,130\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></polyline><polyline points=\"125,30 275,30\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></polyline><circle cx=\"125\" cy=\"130\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></circle><circle cx=\"125\" cy=\"30\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></circle><foreignObject x=\"109\" y=\"80\" width=\"14\" height=\"18\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>O</mi></math></div></foreignObject></svg><figcaption><math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>sgn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">+</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>sgn</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</figcaption></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"!!! definition\n    We say that ``f:X\\mapsto Y`` has *right limit* ``L`` at ``a``, and we write\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to a^+}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\,,\n    ```\n\n    if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n    ```math\n    \\forall \\varepsilon>0,\\exists\\delta\\left(\\varepsilon\\right)>0:a<x<a+\\delta\\implies x\\in X \\wedge \\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n    ```\n\n    Similarly, we say that ``f:X\\mapsto Y`` has *left limit* ``L`` at ``a``, and we write\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to a^-}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\,,\n    ```\n\n    if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n    ```math\n    \\forall \\varepsilon>0,\\exists\\delta\\left(\\varepsilon\\right)>0:a-\\delta<x<a\\implies x\\in X \\wedge \\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n    ```\n\nNote again the dependency of ``\\delta`` on ``\\varepsilon`` and the fact that ``x`` belongs to the domain of ``f``.\n\n!!! example\n    Show that ``\\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\sqrt x=0``.\n\n    Let ``\\varepsilon>0`` be given. If ``x>0``, then ``\\left|\\sqrt x-0\\right|=\\sqrt x``. We can ensure that ``\\sqrt x<\\varepsilon`` by requiring ``x<\\varepsilon^2``. Thus, we can take ``\\delta=\\varepsilon^2`` and the condition of the definition will be satisfied:\n\n    ```math\n    0<x<\\delta=\\varepsilon^2\\implies\\left|\\sqrt x-0\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n    ```\n\n    Therefore, ``\\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\sqrt x=0``.\n\nThe existence of different right and left limits of a function at a point excludes the existence of a limit at that point.\n\n!!! theorem\n    A function ``f`` has limit ``L`` at ``x=a`` if and only if it has both left and right limits there and these one-sided limits are both equal to ``L``:\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\iff\\lim_{x\\to a^-}f\\left(x\\right)=\\lim_{x\\to a^+}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\,.\n    ```\n\n!!! example\n    What one-sided limits does ``g\\left(x\\right)=\\sqrt{1-x^2}`` have at ``x=-1`` and ``x=1``?\n\n    The domain of ``g`` is ``\\left[-1,1\\right]``, so ``g`` is defined only to the right of ``x=-1`` and only to the left of ``x=1``.","output":"","result":"!!! definition\n    We say that ``f:X\\mapsto Y`` has *right limit* ``L`` at ``a``, and we write\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to a^+}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\,,\n    ```\n\n    if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n    ```math\n    \\forall \\varepsilon>0,\\exists\\delta\\left(\\varepsilon\\right)>0:a<x<a+\\delta\\implies x\\in X \\wedge \\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n    ```\n\n    Similarly, we say that ``f:X\\mapsto Y`` has *left limit* ``L`` at ``a``, and we write\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to a^-}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\,,\n    ```\n\n    if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n    ```math\n    \\forall \\varepsilon>0,\\exists\\delta\\left(\\varepsilon\\right)>0:a-\\delta<x<a\\implies x\\in X \\wedge \\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n    ```\n\nNote again the dependency of ``\\delta`` on ``\\varepsilon`` and the fact that ``x`` belongs to the domain of ``f``.\n\n!!! example\n    Show that ``\\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\sqrt x=0``.\n\n    Let ``\\varepsilon>0`` be given. If ``x>0``, then ``\\left|\\sqrt x-0\\right|=\\sqrt x``. We can ensure that ``\\sqrt x<\\varepsilon`` by requiring ``x<\\varepsilon^2``. Thus, we can take ``\\delta=\\varepsilon^2`` and the condition of the definition will be satisfied:\n\n    ```math\n    0<x<\\delta=\\varepsilon^2\\implies\\left|\\sqrt x-0\\right|<\\varepsilon\\,.\n    ```\n\n    Therefore, ``\\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\sqrt x=0``.\n\nThe existence of different right and left limits of a function at a point excludes the existence of a limit at that point.\n\n!!! theorem\n    A function ``f`` has limit ``L`` at ``x=a`` if and only if it has both left and right limits there and these one-sided limits are both equal to ``L``:\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\iff\\lim_{x\\to a^-}f\\left(x\\right)=\\lim_{x\\to a^+}f\\left(x\\right)=L\\,.\n    ```\n\n!!! example\n    What one-sided limits does ``g\\left(x\\right)=\\sqrt{1-x^2}`` have at ``x=-1`` and ``x=1``?\n\n    The domain of ``g`` is ``\\left[-1,1\\right]``, so ``g`` is defined only to the right of ``x=-1`` and only to the left of ``x=1``.","resultHTML":"<aside class=\"markdown-admonition markdown-admonition-definition\"><div class=\"markdown-admonition-title\">Definition</div><div class=\"markdown-admonition-body\"><p>We say that <math><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace=\"0.2222em\" rspace=\"0.2222em\">:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>↦</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></math> has <em>right limit</em> <math><mi>L</mi></math> at <math><mi>a</mi></math>, and we write</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">+</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo></mrow></math>\n<p>if the following condition is satisfied:</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mi>∀</mi><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>∃</mi><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace=\"0.2222em\" rspace=\"0.2222em\">:</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>Similarly, we say that <math><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace=\"0.2222em\" rspace=\"0.2222em\">:</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>↦</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></math> has <em>left limit</em> <math><mi>L</mi></math> at <math><mi>a</mi></math>, and we write</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">−</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo></mrow></math>\n<p>if the following condition is satisfied:</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mi>∀</mi><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>∃</mi><mi>δ</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo lspace=\"0.2222em\" rspace=\"0.2222em\">:</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math></div></aside>\n<p>Note again the dependency of <math><mi>δ</mi></math> on <math><mi>ε</mi></math> and the fact that <math><mi>x</mi></math> belongs to the domain of <math><mi>f</mi></math>.</p>\n<aside class=\"markdown-admonition markdown-admonition-example\"><div class=\"markdown-admonition-title\">Example</div><div class=\"markdown-admonition-body\"><p>Show that <math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">+</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>Let <math><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> be given. If <math><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, then <math><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt></mrow></math>. We can ensure that <math><mrow><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></math> by requiring <math><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. Thus, we can take <math><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> and the condition of the definition will be satisfied:</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>Therefore, <math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">+</mo></msup></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><msqrt><mi>x</mi></msqrt><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p></div></aside>\n<p>The existence of different right and left limits of a function at a point excludes the existence of a limit at that point.</p>\n<aside class=\"markdown-admonition markdown-admonition-theorem\"><div class=\"markdown-admonition-title\">Theorem</div><div class=\"markdown-admonition-body\"><p>A function <math><mi>f</mi></math> has limit <math><mi>L</mi></math> at <math><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> if and only if it has both left and right limits there and these one-sided limits are both equal to <math><mi>L</mi></math>:</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟺</mo><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">−</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><msup><mi>a</mi><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">+</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"markdown-admonition markdown-admonition-example\"><div class=\"markdown-admonition-title\">Example</div><div class=\"markdown-admonition-body\"><p>What one-sided limits does <math><mrow><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> have at <math><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>?</p>\n<p>The domain of <math><mi>g</mi></math> is <math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1,1</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></math>, so <math><mi>g</mi></math> is defined only to the right of <math><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and only to the left of <math><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p></div></aside>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const figure = JSCells.figure({\n    width: 255,\n    height: 160,\n    caption: \"\\\\(\\\\sqrt{1 - x^2}\\\\) has right limit \\\\(0\\\\) at \\\\(-1\\\\) and left limit \\\\(0\\\\) at \\\\(1\\\\)\",\n});\n\nfigure.axes({\n    origin: [125, 130],\n    scale: 100,\n    x: { ticks: [-1, 1] },\n    y: { ticks: [1] },\n});\n\nfigure.plot(\n    (x) => Math.sqrt(Math.max(0, 1 - x ** 2)),\n    { domain: [-1, 1], samples: 201, stroke: \"#ff0000\" },\n);\n\nfor (const x of [-1, 1]) {\n    figure.point(x, 0, {\n        radius: 3,\n        fill: \"#ff0000\",\n        stroke: \"#ff0000\",\n    });\n}\n\nfigure.label(\"O\", { x: 109, y: 130, latex: true, width: 14, height: 18 });\n\nfigure;","output":"","result":"xy−111O1−x2 has right limit 0 at −1 and left limit 0 at 1","resultHTML":"<figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"255\" height=\"160\" viewBox=\"0 0 255 160\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"130\" x2=\"255\" y2=\"130\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><line x1=\"125\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"125\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"255,130 248,126 248,134\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><polygon points=\"125,0 121,7 129,7\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><foreignObject x=\"241\" y=\"100\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"133\" y=\"-2\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>y</mi></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"25\" y1=\"126\" x2=\"25\" y2=\"134\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"21\" y=\"138\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"225\" y1=\"126\" x2=\"225\" y2=\"134\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"221\" y=\"138\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"121\" y1=\"30\" x2=\"129\" y2=\"30\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"101\" y=\"18\" width=\"32\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></div></foreignObject><polyline points=\"25,130 26,115.8932640203341 27,110.10025125786758 28,105.68950843771356 29,102 30,98.775010008008 31,95.88255578153603 32,93.24404810102178 33,90.80816411546915 34,88.53917511674425 35,86.41101056459327 36,84.40394753928801 37,82.50263165184833 38,80.6948278575158 39,78.9705967113077 40,77.3217312357363 41,75.74136013499785 42,74.22366093046264 43,72.76364791498327 44,71.35701235441701 45,70.00000000000001 46,68.68931577612267 47,67.42204861135194 48,66.19561143620291 49,65.00769276291231 50,63.85621722338523 51,62.73931311679905 52,61.655285500632885 53,60.60259370841011 54,59.57983243416699 55,58.585715714571506 56,57.61906328320971 57,56.67878888070655 58,55.763890188130134 59.000000000000014,54.873440116028206 60,54.00657923214669 61,53.1625091508058 62,52.3404867385843 63,51.539819016267884 64,50.759858657369875 65,50 66,49.2596755022622 67,48.538352582334795 68,47.83553079341412 69,47.150739291168094 70,46.483534557549675 71,45.83349834999676 72,45.20023584938458 73,44.58337398374951 74,43.982559907888444 75,43.39745962155614 76,42.82775671120996 77,42.273151202154764 78,41.733358509570564 79,41.20810847830754 80,40.697144502541235 81,40.200222717425405 82,39.717111255786676 83,39.247589563692586 84,38.79144776941145 85,38.34848610088321 86,37.91851434734559 87,37.501351361222575 88,37.09682459678787 89,36.70476968247519 90,36.32503002402403 91,35.95745643593003 92,35.60190679891886 93,35.2582457413839 94,34.926344342925375 95,34.606079858305435 96,34.29733546029139 97,34 98,33.71396778348377 99,33.439138363413505 100,33.17541634481458 101,32.92271120390723 102,32.68093711918512 103,32.45001281394241 104,32.229861409528525 105,32.02041028867288 106,31.82159096827857 107,31.633338981136504 108,31.455593766058954 109,31.28829856597548 110,31.13140033357405 111,30.984849644107484 112,30.848600615019052 113,30.722610832073144 114,30.60684128170591 115,30.501256289338002 116,30.405823463417292 117,30.320513644983095 118,30.245300862565884 119,30.180162292257762 120,30.12507822280911 121,30.080032025625627 122,30.04501012955882 123,30.020002000400098 124,30.005000125006248 125,30 126,30.005000125006248 127,30.020002000400098 128,30.04501012955882 129,30.080032025625627 130,30.12507822280911 131,30.180162292257762 132,30.245300862565884 133,30.320513644983095 134,30.405823463417292 135,30.501256289338002 136,30.60684128170591 137,30.72261083207316 138,30.848600615019052 139,30.984849644107484 140,31.13140033357405 141,31.288298565975467 142,31.455593766058954 143,31.633338981136504 144,31.82159096827857 145,32.02041028867288 146,32.229861409528525 147,32.45001281394241 148,32.68093711918512 149,32.92271120390723 150,33.17541634481458 151,33.439138363413505 152,33.71396778348377 153,34 154,34.29733546029139 155,34.606079858305435 156,34.926344342925375 157,35.25824574138392 158,35.60190679891886 159,35.95745643593003 160,36.32503002402403 161,36.7047696824752 162,37.09682459678787 163,37.501351361222575 164,37.91851434734558 165,38.34848610088319 166,38.79144776941145 167,39.24758956369257 168,39.717111255786676 169,40.200222717425405 170,40.697144502541235 171,41.20810847830754 172,41.733358509570564 173,42.273151202154764 174,42.82775671120996 175,43.39745962155614 176,43.982559907888444 177,44.58337398374951 178,45.20023584938458 179,45.83349834999676 180,46.483534557549675 181,47.150739291168094 182,47.83553079341412 183,48.538352582334795 184,49.2596755022622 185,50 186,50.759858657369875 187,51.539819016267884 188,52.3404867385843 189,53.1625091508058 190,54.00657923214668 191,54.873440116028206 192,55.763890188130134 193,56.67878888070655 194,57.61906328320971 195,58.585715714571506 196,59.57983243416699 197,60.60259370841011 198,61.655285500632885 199,62.73931311679905 200,63.85621722338523 201,65.00769276291231 202,66.19561143620291 203,67.42204861135194 204,68.68931577612267 205,70.00000000000001 206,71.35701235441701 207,72.76364791498327 208,74.22366093046264 209,75.74136013499788 210,77.32173123573632 211,78.97059671130772 212,80.69482785751583 213,82.50263165184832 214,84.403947539288 215,86.41101056459325 216,88.53917511674422 217,90.80816411546913 218,93.24404810102178 219,95.88255578153603 220,98.775010008008 221,102 222,105.68950843771356 223,110.10025125786758 224,115.8932640203341 225,130\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></polyline><circle cx=\"25\" cy=\"130\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#ff0000\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></circle><circle cx=\"225\" cy=\"130\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#ff0000\" stroke=\"#ff0000\"></circle><foreignObject x=\"109\" y=\"130\" width=\"14\" height=\"18\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>O</mi></math></div></foreignObject></svg><figcaption><math><msqrt><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></math> has right limit <math><mn>0</mn></math> at <math><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and left limit <math><mn>0</mn></math> at <math><mn>1</mn></math></figcaption></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"!!! example\n\tAs can be seen in the figure,\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to-1^+}g\\left(x\\right)=0\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad\\lim_{x\\to1^-}g\\left(x\\right)=0\n    ```\n\n    ``g`` has no left limit or limit at ``x=-1``, no right limit or limit at ``x=1``.\n","output":"","result":"!!! example\n\tAs can be seen in the figure,\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to-1^+}g\\left(x\\right)=0\\quad\\textrm{and}\\quad\\lim_{x\\to1^-}g\\left(x\\right)=0\n    ```\n\n    ``g`` has no left limit or limit at ``x=-1``, no right limit or limit at ``x=1``.\n","resultHTML":"<aside class=\"markdown-admonition markdown-admonition-example\"><div class=\"markdown-admonition-title\">Example</div><div class=\"markdown-admonition-body\"><p>As can be seen in the figure,</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">+</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">−</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>\n<p><math><mi>g</mi></math> has no left limit or limit at <math><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"false\">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, no right limit or limit at <math><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p></div></aside>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Rules and Theorems about Limits\n\nThe following rules make it easy to calculate limits and one-sided limits of many kinds of functions when we know some elementary limits.\n\n!!! theorem \"Limit Rules\"\n    If ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L``, ``\\lim_{x\\to a}g\\left(x\\right)=M`` and ``k`` is a constant, then\n\n    1. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=L+M`` (limit of a sum)\n    2. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left(f\\left(x\\right)-g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=L-M`` (limit of a difference)\n    3. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)g\\left(x\\right)=LM`` (limit of a product)\n    4. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}kf\\left(x\\right)=kL`` (limit of a multiple)\n    5. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f\\left(x\\right)}{g\\left(x\\right)}=\\frac{L}{M}\\textrm{ if }M\\ne0`` (limit of quotient)\n    6. If ``m\\in \\mathbb{Z}`` and ``n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0``, then ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left(f\\left(x\\right)\\right)^\\frac{m}{n}=L^\\frac{m}{n}``, provided ``L>0`` if ``n`` is even and ``L\\ne0`` if ``m <0``. (limit of a power)\n    7. If ``f\\left(x\\right)\\le g\\left(x\\right)`` on an interval containing ``a`` in its interior, then ``L\\le M``. (order is preserved)\n\nRules 1—6 are also valid for one-sided limits. So is rule 7, under the assumption that ``f\\left(x\\right)\\le g\\left(x\\right)`` on an open interval extending from ``a`` in the appropriate direction.\n\n!!! proof \"limit of a sum\"\n    Let ``\\varepsilon > 0`` be given.\n\n    We want to find a strict positive number ``\\delta`` such that\n\n    ```math\n    0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta\\implies\\left|\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)-\\left(L+M\\right)\\right|<\\varepsilon\n    ```\n\n    Observe that\n\n    ```math\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\left|\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)-\\left(L+M\\right)\\right|=&\\left|\\left(f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right)+\\left(g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right)\\right|\\\\\n    \\le&\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|+\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|\n    \\end{aligned}\n    ```\n\n    by the triangle inequality.\n\n    Since ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L`` and ``\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}`` is a strict positive number, there exists a number ``\\delta_1`` such that\n\n    ```math\n    0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_1\\implies\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}\\,.\n    ```\n\n    Similarly, since ``\\lim_{x\\to a}g\\left(x\\right)=M`` and ``\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}`` is a strict positive number, there exists a number ``\\delta_2`` such that\n\n    ```math\n    0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_2\\implies\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}\\,.\n    ```\n\n    Let ``\\delta=\\min\\left\\lbrace\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\right\\rbrace``. If ``0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta``, then ``\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_1``, so ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}``, and ``\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_2``, so ``\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}``. Therefore,\n\n    ```math\n    \\left|\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)-\\left(L+M\\right)\\right|\\le\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|+\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}+\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}=\\varepsilon\\,.\n    ```\n\n    This shows that ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=L+M``.\n\n!!! example\n    Find ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{x^2+x+4}{x^3-2x^2+7}``.\n\n    The expression ``\\frac{x^2+x+4}{x^3-2x^2+7}`` is formed by combining the basic functions ``x`` and ``c`` (constant) using addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division. The previous theorem assures us that the limit of such a combination is the same combination of the limits ``a`` and ``c`` of the basic functions, provided the denominator does not have limit zero. Thus,\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{x^2+x+4}{x^3-2x^2+7}=\\frac{a^2+a+4}{a^3-2a^2+7}\\quad\\textrm{provided }a^3-2a^2+7\\ne0\n    ```\n\nThe result of the example can be generalized as a direct corollary.\n\n!!! corollary\n    If ``a\\in \\mathbb{R}`` and\n\n    1. ``P\\left(x\\right)`` is a polynomial, then\n\n       ```math\n       \\lim_{x\\to a}P\\left(x\\right)=P\\left(a\\right)\n       ```\n\n    2. ``P\\left(x\\right)`` and ``Q\\left(x\\right)`` are polynomials and ``Q\\left(a\\right)\\ne 0``, then\n\n       ```math\n       \\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{P\\left(x\\right)}{Q\\left(x\\right)}=\\frac{P\\left(a\\right)}{Q\\left(a\\right)}\\,.\n       ```\n\nThe following theorem will enable us to calculate some very important limits in subsequent chapters. It is called the *squeeze theorem* because it refers to a function ``g`` whose values are squeezed between the values of two other functions that have the same limit ``L`` at a point ``a``. Being trapped between the values of two functions that approach ``L``, the values of ``g`` must also approach ``L``.","output":"","result":"## Rules and Theorems about Limits\n\nThe following rules make it easy to calculate limits and one-sided limits of many kinds of functions when we know some elementary limits.\n\n!!! theorem \"Limit Rules\"\n    If ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L``, ``\\lim_{x\\to a}g\\left(x\\right)=M`` and ``k`` is a constant, then\n\n    1. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=L+M`` (limit of a sum)\n    2. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left(f\\left(x\\right)-g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=L-M`` (limit of a difference)\n    3. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)g\\left(x\\right)=LM`` (limit of a product)\n    4. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}kf\\left(x\\right)=kL`` (limit of a multiple)\n    5. ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{f\\left(x\\right)}{g\\left(x\\right)}=\\frac{L}{M}\\textrm{ if }M\\ne0`` (limit of quotient)\n    6. If ``m\\in \\mathbb{Z}`` and ``n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0``, then ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left(f\\left(x\\right)\\right)^\\frac{m}{n}=L^\\frac{m}{n}``, provided ``L>0`` if ``n`` is even and ``L\\ne0`` if ``m <0``. (limit of a power)\n    7. If ``f\\left(x\\right)\\le g\\left(x\\right)`` on an interval containing ``a`` in its interior, then ``L\\le M``. (order is preserved)\n\nRules 1—6 are also valid for one-sided limits. So is rule 7, under the assumption that ``f\\left(x\\right)\\le g\\left(x\\right)`` on an open interval extending from ``a`` in the appropriate direction.\n\n!!! proof \"limit of a sum\"\n    Let ``\\varepsilon > 0`` be given.\n\n    We want to find a strict positive number ``\\delta`` such that\n\n    ```math\n    0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta\\implies\\left|\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)-\\left(L+M\\right)\\right|<\\varepsilon\n    ```\n\n    Observe that\n\n    ```math\n    \\begin{aligned}\n    \\left|\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)-\\left(L+M\\right)\\right|=&\\left|\\left(f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right)+\\left(g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right)\\right|\\\\\n    \\le&\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|+\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|\n    \\end{aligned}\n    ```\n\n    by the triangle inequality.\n\n    Since ``\\lim_{x\\to a}f\\left(x\\right)=L`` and ``\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}`` is a strict positive number, there exists a number ``\\delta_1`` such that\n\n    ```math\n    0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_1\\implies\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}\\,.\n    ```\n\n    Similarly, since ``\\lim_{x\\to a}g\\left(x\\right)=M`` and ``\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}`` is a strict positive number, there exists a number ``\\delta_2`` such that\n\n    ```math\n    0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_2\\implies\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}\\,.\n    ```\n\n    Let ``\\delta=\\min\\left\\lbrace\\delta_1,\\delta_2\\right\\rbrace``. If ``0<\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta``, then ``\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_1``, so ``\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}``, and ``\\left|x-a\\right|<\\delta_2``, so ``\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}``. Therefore,\n\n    ```math\n    \\left|\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)-\\left(L+M\\right)\\right|\\le\\left|f\\left(x\\right)-L\\right|+\\left|g\\left(x\\right)-M\\right|<\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}+\\frac{\\varepsilon}{2}=\\varepsilon\\,.\n    ```\n\n    This shows that ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\left(f\\left(x\\right)+g\\left(x\\right)\\right)=L+M``.\n\n!!! example\n    Find ``\\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{x^2+x+4}{x^3-2x^2+7}``.\n\n    The expression ``\\frac{x^2+x+4}{x^3-2x^2+7}`` is formed by combining the basic functions ``x`` and ``c`` (constant) using addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division. The previous theorem assures us that the limit of such a combination is the same combination of the limits ``a`` and ``c`` of the basic functions, provided the denominator does not have limit zero. Thus,\n\n    ```math\n    \\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{x^2+x+4}{x^3-2x^2+7}=\\frac{a^2+a+4}{a^3-2a^2+7}\\quad\\textrm{provided }a^3-2a^2+7\\ne0\n    ```\n\nThe result of the example can be generalized as a direct corollary.\n\n!!! corollary\n    If ``a\\in \\mathbb{R}`` and\n\n    1. ``P\\left(x\\right)`` is a polynomial, then\n\n       ```math\n       \\lim_{x\\to a}P\\left(x\\right)=P\\left(a\\right)\n       ```\n\n    2. ``P\\left(x\\right)`` and ``Q\\left(x\\right)`` are polynomials and ``Q\\left(a\\right)\\ne 0``, then\n\n       ```math\n       \\lim_{x\\to a}\\frac{P\\left(x\\right)}{Q\\left(x\\right)}=\\frac{P\\left(a\\right)}{Q\\left(a\\right)}\\,.\n       ```\n\nThe following theorem will enable us to calculate some very important limits in subsequent chapters. It is called the *squeeze theorem* because it refers to a function ``g`` whose values are squeezed between the values of two other functions that have the same limit ``L`` at a point ``a``. Being trapped between the values of two functions that approach ``L``, the values of ``g`` must also approach ``L``.","resultHTML":"<h2 id=\"md-x8vise-911-1ic99ft-rules-and-theorems-about-limits\">Rules and Theorems about Limits</h2>\n<p>The following rules make it easy to calculate limits and one-sided limits of many kinds of functions when we know some elementary limits.</p>\n<aside class=\"markdown-admonition markdown-admonition-theorem\"><div class=\"markdown-admonition-title\">Theorem: Limit Rules</div><div class=\"markdown-admonition-body\"><p>If <math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, <math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> and <math><mi>k</mi></math> is a constant, then</p>\n<ol>\n<li><math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> (limit of a sum)\n</li>\n<li><math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> (limit of a difference)\n</li>\n<li><math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> (limit of a product)\n</li>\n<li><math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>k</mi><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> (limit of a multiple)\n</li>\n<li><math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>L</mi><mi>M</mi></mfrac><mtext> if </mtext><mi>M</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (limit of quotient)\n</li>\n<li>If <math><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math> and <math><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, then <math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mfrac><mi scriptlevel=\"2\" style=\"math-depth:2;\">m</mi><mi scriptlevel=\"2\" style=\"math-depth:2;\">n</mi></mfrac></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mfrac class=\"tml-sml-pad\"><mi scriptlevel=\"2\" style=\"math-depth:2;\">m</mi><mi scriptlevel=\"2\" style=\"math-depth:2;\">n</mi></mfrac></msup></mrow></math>, provided <math><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> if <math><mi>n</mi></math> is even and <math><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> if <math><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. (limit of a power)\n</li>\n<li>If <math><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> on an interval containing <math><mi>a</mi></math> in its interior, then <math><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>. (order is preserved)\n</li>\n</ol></div></aside>\n<p>Rules 1—6 are also valid for one-sided limits. So is rule 7, under the assumption that <math><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> on an open interval extending from <math><mi>a</mi></math> in the appropriate direction.</p>\n<aside class=\"markdown-admonition markdown-admonition-proof\"><div class=\"markdown-admonition-title\">Proof: limit of a sum</div><div class=\"markdown-admonition-body\"><p>Let <math><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> be given.</p>\n<p>We want to find a strict positive number <math><mi>δ</mi></math> such that</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>Observe that</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mtable displaystyle=\"true\" class=\"tml-jot\"><mtr><mtd class=\"tml-right\" style=\"padding-left:0em;padding-right:0em;\"><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class=\"tml-left\" style=\"padding-left:0em;padding-right:0em;\"><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class=\"tml-right\" style=\"padding-left:0em;padding-right:0em;\"><mo>≤</mo></mtd><mtd class=\"tml-left\" style=\"padding-left:0em;padding-right:0em;\"><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></math>\n<p>by the triangle inequality.</p>\n<p>Since <math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> and <math><mfrac><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> is a strict positive number, there exists a number <math><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> such that</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>Similarly, since <math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> and <math><mfrac><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math> is a strict positive number, there exists a number <math><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> such that</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"0.2778em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>Let <math><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">{</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">}</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. If <math><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></math>, then <math><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>, so <math><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></math>, and <math><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>, so <math><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></math>. Therefore,</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>ε</mi><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>This shows that <math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"markdown-admonition markdown-admonition-example\"><div class=\"markdown-admonition-title\">Example</div><div class=\"markdown-admonition-body\"><p>Find <math><mrow><msub><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p>\n<p>The expression <math><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mfrac></math> is formed by combining the basic functions <math><mi>x</mi></math> and <math><mi>c</mi></math> (constant) using addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division. The previous theorem assures us that the limit of such a combination is the same combination of the limits <math><mi>a</mi></math> and <math><mi>c</mi></math> of the basic functions, provided the denominator does not have limit zero. Thus,</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></mfrac><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mtext>provided </mtext><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></div></aside>\n<p>The result of the example can be generalized as a direct corollary.</p>\n<aside class=\"markdown-admonition markdown-admonition-corollary\"><div class=\"markdown-admonition-title\">Corollary</div><div class=\"markdown-admonition-body\"><p>If <math><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></math> and</p>\n<ol>\n<li><math><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> is a polynomial, then\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math>\n</li>\n<li><math><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and <math><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></math> are polynomials and <math><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, then\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>P</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math>\n</li>\n</ol></div></aside>\n<p>The following theorem will enable us to calculate some very important limits in subsequent chapters. It is called the <em>squeeze theorem</em> because it refers to a function <math><mi>g</mi></math> whose values are squeezed between the values of two other functions that have the same limit <math><mi>L</mi></math> at a point <math><mi>a</mi></math>. Being trapped between the values of two functions that approach <math><mi>L</mi></math>, the values of <math><mi>g</mi></math> must also approach <math><mi>L</mi></math>.</p>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const scale = 40;\nconst red = \"#ff0000\";\nconst green = \"#008000\";\nconst blue = \"#4169e1\";\n\nconst figure = JSCells.figure({\n    width: 6 * scale,\n    height: 4.5 * scale+10,\n    caption: \"The graph of g is squeezed between those of f and h.\",\n});\n\nfigure.axes({\n    origin: [scale, 4 * scale],\n    scale,\n    x: { ticks: [{ value: 3, label: \"a\", length: 1 }] },\n    y: { ticks: [{ value: 2, label: \"L\", length: 1 }] },\n});\n\nfigure.line()\n    .x1(4 * scale).y1(4 * scale)\n    .x2(4 * scale).y2(2 * scale)\n    .stroke(\"#000000\")\n    .setAttribute(\"stroke-dasharray\", \"3\");\n\nfigure.line()\n    .x1(scale).y1(2 * scale)\n    .x2(4 * scale).y2(2 * scale)\n    .stroke(\"#000000\")\n    .setAttribute(\"stroke-dasharray\", \"3\");\n\nconst plotOptions = { domain: [-1, 5], samples: 601 };\n\nfigure.plot((x) => 2 + 0.3 * (x - 3) ** 2, {\n    ...plotOptions,\n    stroke: green,\n});\nfigure.plot((x) => 1 + Math.cos(x - 3), {\n    ...plotOptions,\n    stroke: blue,\n});\nfigure.plot((x) => 2 - 0.1 * (x - 3) ** 2, {\n    ...plotOptions,\n    stroke: red,\n});\n\nfigure.point(3, 2, {\n    radius: 3,\n    fill: \"#ffffff\",\n    stroke: red,\n});\n\nfigure.label(\"\\\\color[HTML]{#ff0000}y = g(x)\", {\n    x: scale,\n    y: 1.9 * scale,\n    latex: true,\n});\nfigure.label(\"\\\\color[HTML]{#008000}y = h(x)\", {\n    x: scale,\n    y: 1.2 * scale,\n    latex: true,\n});\nfigure.label(\"\\\\color[HTML]{#4169e1}y = f(x)\", {\n    x: scale,\n    y: 2.6 * scale,\n    latex: true,\n});\n\nfigure;","output":"","result":"xyaLy=g(x)y=h(x)y=f(x)The graph of g is squeezed between those of f and h.","resultHTML":"<figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"240\" height=\"190\" viewBox=\"0 0 240 190\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"240\" y2=\"160\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"0\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"190\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><polygon points=\"240,160 233,156 233,164\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><polygon points=\"40,0 36,7 44,7\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\"></polygon><foreignObject x=\"226\" y=\"130\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"48\" y=\"-2\" width=\"14\" height=\"28\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>y</mi></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"156\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"164\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"153\" y=\"168\" width=\"14\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>a</mi></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"36\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"44\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#000000\"></line><foreignObject x=\"26\" y=\"68\" width=\"14\" height=\"32\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>L</mi></math></div></foreignObject><line x1=\"160\" y1=\"160\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-dasharray=\"3\"></line><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"80\" x2=\"160\" y2=\"80\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-dasharray=\"3\"></line><polyline poin